hey 42 here i've heard it said that you  have to speculate to accumulate  and that's absolutely true  but there's a flip side to that catchy  phrase speculating is also a pretty  great way to lose every penny you own  if you're ever unlucky enough to find  yourself in that unfortunate position  there are two main ways of dealing with  your loss  either you can throw in the towel and  accept the crappy hand fate has dealt  you or you can pick yourself up dust  yourself down and declare yourself the  supreme leader of whichever country you  happen to live in  one person who opted for that second  option which i admit was a bit of a  curveball was a formally successful san  francisco businessman who bankrupted  himself by speculating on of all things  the price of rice  and what happened next is quite simply  one of the weirdest and most  heartwarming tales i've ever covered on  this channel  this is the story of joshua norton the  first and last emperor of the united  states of america  now i can guarantee that none of you  have heard the story of the 195 million  dollar bullet back in 1964 the most  popular artist in the world andy warhol  had just finished a series of four  canvases depicting marilyn monroe in his  famous pop art style so when a  photographer friend asked if he could  shoot the portraits he assumed she was  talking about taking photos imagine his  surprise when she pulled a pistol out of  her purse and fired a single bullet  piercing three of the four canvases  right through marilyn's forehead fast  forward to a few weeks ago and one of  the portraits shot blue marilyn sells at  auction for 195 million dollars smashing  the auction record for a 20th century  artwork it sounds unbelievable but this  track record of contemporary arts  delivering insane returns is exactly  what inspired a unicorn startup called  masterworks to open the art market to  the public at large speaking of returns  each of the three paintings masterworks  has sold so far after offering them to  their members have returned over 30 net  now legally i have to add past  performance is no guarantee of future  results but i've talked about  masterworks on the tunnel before because  i think what they're doing is genius the  art market isn't reserved for the ultra  wealthy in fact members of the 42  community have already invested with  masterworks with the stock market being  crazy recently now's the perfect time to  diversify your portfolio and masterworks  is how i'm choosing to do that and  luckily for you masterworks has provided  my viewers with priority access so go  check them out and see the new offering  they have on their platform using the  link in the description below thanks to  masterworks for sponsoring this video  exactly when joshua abraham norton was  born has been lost to the sands of time  but most norton scholars believe it was  probably in early february of 1818.  that being the case norton would have  been just two years old when his parents  packed their bags and left their home in  england behind to set sail for south  africa where the british had recently  established a colony  not much is known about norton's  formative years in south africa  nor what it was that convinced him to  leave it 25 years later  but what we do know is that by the late  1840s norton had packed up his life in  africa and made his way to america and  specifically san francisco  norton was a bright guy and talented  businessman and within the space of just  a few short years he'd proven just how  apt america's reputation as the land of  opportunity really was by amassing a  good chunk of cash through a series of  successful real estate ventures and a  certain knack for commodities trading  all told norton accumulated some 250 000  in those early years  that's about 10 million dollars in  today's money and it seems that being  rich was just as op back in the 19th  century as it is today  because norton's wealth helped him rise  to become one of the most important and  respected figures in the whole of san  francisco he was invited to the fanciest  parties and consulted on the most  important topics of the day  he wore fine clothes ate it the best  restaurants and lived in a grand mtv  cribs worthy mansion  but just when it seemed norton was  poised to become a playboy of  gatsby-like proportions  one bad deal brought everything he'd  built crashing down around him  and all it took was 90 000 kilograms  of rice  around the time norton arrived in  america famine was spreading across  large parts of china  in an attempt to avoid an all-out  catastrophe a ban was placed on the  export of rice a move that saw its price  rocket around the world  so when a ship laden with peruvian rice  sailed into san francisco bay in late  1852  not long after the chinese export ban  was imposed joshua norton's eyes did  that spinning dollar signs thing you  sometimes see in the cartoons acting  fast he snapped up the entire shipment  for twenty five thousand dollars about a  million in today's money  then he simply sat back lit a giant  cigar  probably and waited for the price of  rice to saw  and that's exactly what it did  for all of about 24 hours  that's how long it took for the next  ship stuffed filter bursting with  peruvian rice to arrive in san francisco  and over the next couple of weeks  several more ships followed  not only was there now far more rice  available than norton had anticipated  the lot he purchased was pretty much the  worst of them all in terms of quality  far from earning a tidy profit in less  than a month norton's investment had  lost 90 percent of its original value  as i've said norton was a rich man by  this time and had he simply accepted his  loss and moved on  i probably wouldn't be making this video  but  as it happened  he'd only actually paid a 2 000  upfront fee for the rice with the rest  of the contract due at a later date and  nowadays it was clear that the price was  anything but right  norton flatly refused to settle the  balance  a two-year legal battle followed with  the case eventually ending up in front  of the california supreme court  norton lost and when the damages legal  fees and lost earnings had been tutted  up  he was left with the square root of  bugarol  well that and a shitload of rice  the effect all of this had on norton was  considerable the lavish parties and  expensive cloves were gone  as was the swanky san francisco mansion  he was flat broke and his friends  influence and good standing were a  distant memory  nobody really knows whether the whole  ordeal simply pushed norton's sanity to  breaking point  or if what came next was the inevitable  evolution of a man who was apparently a  lot more eccentric than anyone had  noticed during the good times  whichever it was it soon became clear  that norton had no intention of  accepting his new lower station in life  [Music]  in 1858 several years after the ruinous  rice write-off when a penniless norton  was living in a boarding house he  decided to run for congress perhaps  unsurprisingly this somewhat ambitious  career move ended in failure  but norton didn't let that hold him back  if the good people of the united states  of america didn't have the wherewithal  to elect him to a position of power  he was simply going to have to take the  decision out of their hands  in the autumn of the following year  norton arrived at the offices of the san  francisco daily evening bulletin  armed with a handwritten letter  this is what it said  at the preemptory request and desire of  a large majority of the citizens of the  united states i joshua norton formerly  of algoa bay cape of good hope and now  for the last nine years and ten months  past of san francisco california  declare and proclaim myself  emperor of these united states  after he'd stopped laughing the editor  at the san francisco daily evening  bulletin did something quite unexpected  he agreed to publish norton's letter  the decision was motivated more by the  comedy value of norton's preposterous  statement than the fact the editor had  just been given a world exclusive by the  usa's first ever emperor  but even so the declaration was  published and the rest is history  norton's 21-year reign as the  self-proclaimed emperor of the united  states of america  had just begun  the first thing on the new emperor's  agenda was to sort out the rabble who'd  been doing such a crappy job running the  country before him  norton considered congress to be full of  fraud and corruption he'd have fit  writing in 2022 wouldn't he and so one  of his first official decrees was to  abolish it entirely  three months later when it had become  clear that the big wigs in washington  had no intention of packing up and going  home  no matter what the crazy guy in san  francisco thought  emperor norton realized it was time to  get serious and so he ordered the man  they called old stuff and feathers  commanding general of the us army  winfield scott to forcibly remove  washington's elected officials from  their posts unfortunately this perfectly  reasonable royal decree was ignored too  now to tell the truth the majority of  emperor norton's 250-plus edicts  delivered over his two decades in charge  of the united states  were completely ignored  the majority but remarkably enough  not all  i'll get to that in a minute  norton took his job as leader of the  free world incredibly seriously by day  he could be seen doing his rounds of san  francisco inspecting the local  infrastructure making state visits to  the city parks and visiting the office  of newspapers to dish out more decrees  and proclamations he looked the part too  norton was never seen out of full royal  attire  which consisted of a rather fetching  blue uniform with gold-plated epaulettes  a beaver fur hat topped with a jaunty  peacock feather and either a ceremonial  saber or a stately cane depending on how  badass he was feeling on that particular  day  notson was so serious about his role as  monarch that he even issued his own  currency premisery notes in  denominations of 50 cents to 10  plenty of local businesses actually  accepted them too  norton's made up money was good enough  for many of san francisco's best  restaurants where he ate on a nightly  basis and a large number of shops and  restaurants hung signs in their windows  reading by appointments to his majesty  emperor norton the first  you might be thinking that any business  owner who accepted fictional currency  from a made-up king was clearly an  absolute mug  if so you'd be dead wrong  today the few surviving examples of  emperor norton's premisery notes sell at  auction for up to 15 000  apiece  with so much time having passed since  norton's day it's difficult to say for  certain whether or not the emperor of  the united states  was simply an eccentric man who'd fallen  on hard times  or someone suffering from genuine mental  illness who truly believed he was  running the country  but the latter is certainly a  possibility  one that was considered even back in  norton's day  he was once arrested by a member of san  francisco's police force who planned to  have norton tried for insanity it would  have been interesting to know what an  expert might have made of norton but no  assessment was ever carried out because  the arrest triggered a huge backlash  amongst the people of the city  not to mention several local newspapers  nawsom was released soon afterwards and  issued with a formal apology  not one to hold a grudge norton  responded in kind but stirring an  official pardon on the police officer  who'd arrested him  from that day forward norton was saluted  by every single policeman who passed him  in the streets of san francisco  you have to admit the whole thing was a  genuinely weird situation  on the one hand people obviously knew  that emperor norton wasn't actually  running the country  but for the most part he was treated as  though he was doing exactly that  and it wasn't just the locals in san  fran who were playing along if you were  to take a look at the 1870 u.s census  you'd find norton's official occupation  listed  as emperor  and it's even rumored that kamiyama had  v  at the time the king of hawaii grew so  annoyed with the us government that he  formally recognized norton as the one  true leader of the united states by the  way as a completely random aside if the  name kamiyama had v sounds oddly  familiar to you it may be that you're a  dragon ball z fan  goku's famous finishing move was for  reasons that are hard to explain named  after the hawaiian king  anyway over the years norton became such  an influential figure in his own strange  way  that newspaper started to print fake  declarations from the emperor in an  attempt to achieve their own goals  so many of these fake proclamations were  printed that these days it's difficult  to know which ones were actually his  but the thing is  for all his pomp circumstance and  possible insanity emperor norton  actually had some bloody good ideas  for example in one decree he called for  the formation of a global league of  nations to work towards such noble goals  as world peace an organization very much  like the one norton described was  founded in 1945  more than six decades after his death  today we know it as the united nations  in the 1870s norton also decreed that  either a suspension bridge or a tunnel  be built to improve the connection  between oakland and san francisco  almost 60 years later the now world  famous bay bridge opened to the public  for the first time  and the trans bay tube a tunnel  connecting oakland and san francisco  that like the bay bridge was built  exactly where norton proposed it should  be  came about 40 years after that  norton was also light years ahead of his  time when it came to things like  equality and religion he decreed that  people of all skin colours be allowed to  ride in streetcars and attend schools a  truly radical idea in mid 19th century  america he believed in votes for women  something that didn't actually happen  until 1920  and he made declarations advocating the  separation of church and state and  forbidding conflict between religions  all things considered it might not have  been such a bad thing  if emperor norton actually had been the  leader of the free world  but sadly even the most illustrious of  royal reigns must someday come to an end  and for norton that day came on the 8th  of january 1880 when he collapsed whilst  walking the streets of san francisco the  city he loved and that had come to love  him he died before help could arrive  the funeral was held two days later and  according to newspaper articles printed  at the time as many as 10 000 people  came to pay their respects to the fallen  emperor  in keeping with norton's almost mythical  status  it had long been speculated that he was  actually fabulously wealthy and merely  pretending to be a crazy old guy to  throw people off the scent  there were even rumors that he was the  son of napoleon iii the last monarch to  rule over france  he wasn't  obviously  and one look at his shabby apartment  laid those stories of fabulous wealth to  rest  despite his frankly remarkable life and  the surprising level of power he wielded  norton died a pauper with just a couple  of dollars to his name but his story is  such a strange one that it's inspired  the work of countless writers and  creatives ever since you can find norton  doing his crazy thing in the pages of  novels by mark twain and robert louis  stevenson  in the cells of comics written by neil  gaiman and in a whole host of different  tv series and films his name checks in  popular culture are admittedly somewhat  less frequent than they used to be but  for my money that's a shame when life  gave joshua norton lemons  he made lemonade  and then spent the next two decades  insisting he was leader of the free  world  and the best thing is  somehow against all the odds  it kind of worked  thanks for watching
 

